const faq = [
  { q: 'Are ravens common in Scandinavia?', a: 'Common Ravens are present throughout Scandinavia, though their distribution and density vary significantly. In northern Sweden (Norrland), Norway (beyond the Arctic Circle), and Finnish Lapland, ravens are abundant, year-round forest and tundra residents closely associated with reindeer herding culture. In southern Scandinavia — Denmark, Skåne, lowland Sweden — ravens are present but less common, historically persecuted and only gradually recovering. Norway\'s raven population is estimated at approximately 10,000–15,000 breeding pairs. Sweden\'s population is similar in scale, concentrated in the boreal north. Finland has a similar pattern of abundance in the north, relative scarcity in agricultural south.' },
  { q: 'Do ravens follow reindeer in Scandinavia?', a: 'Yes — the raven-reindeer association is ecologically significant in Scandinavia and Finland. Sámi reindeer herders have documented this relationship for centuries: ravens follow reindeer herds, feeding on carcasses of animals that die naturally in winter, on afterbirth during calving, and on food scraps around herding camps. Ravens are considered both a nuisance (scavenging from stored food, harassing weak animals) and a useful indicator by some herders — their gathering at a location can signal a dead animal. The raven figures prominently in Sámi oral tradition precisely because of this close ecological relationship.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens protected in Scandinavia?', a: 'Ravens are protected in Sweden, Norway, and Finland under national wildlife protection legislation and the EU Birds Directive. In Norway, historically extensive raven persecution (bounty systems existed until the mid-20th century) has ended, and populations have recovered. Limited depredation licences can be issued in some circumstances for ravens causing documented livestock damage. In Sweden, the raven was classified as a game species historically; it is now fully protected. Denmark has the smallest raven population in Scandinavia, with ongoing monitoring of its limited breeding pairs in Jutland.' },
];
